<!-- =================================================================
     OPTION B — CONSOLIDATED REFERENCE LIST
     Tour prose stays clean; all cheatsheet links live in a single
     "structures you'll use" list at the start of the build.
     ================================================================= -->

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 5:10-5:35 Build a Worksheet Lesson                -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-build-lesson">
    <title>5:10-5:35 Worksheet Lesson</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This is the main event. The goal: replicate a short product-rule worksheet in your own project, using the structures you met in Guided Practice plus a handful of new ones we will point out along the way.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="bl-tour">
      <title>Tour the target</title>

      <p>
        Open the rendered worksheet (projected at the front, or the link on the slide) and work through it the way a student would. Try the first exercise. Expand an answer knowl. Click a multiple-choice option. Get a feel for what you are about to build.
      </p>

      <p>
        As you read, notice the pieces:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The whole page is a single worksheet, split into two pages so it paginates nicely in print.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The bulleted list at the top is a structured objectives block — a special structure for stating what the worksheet teaches.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The boxed formula is a definition with an ID, which lets the later exercises link back to it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Why we need it</q> is one exercise with three sub-parts, each with its own answer knowl.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The worked example uses a cross-reference to point back to the definition.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Spot the derivative</q> is a multiple-choice exercise — each option has its own feedback knowl.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="bl-build">
      <title>Build it</title>

      <p>
        Open a fresh file in your project and replicate the worksheet. To spare you the tedium of retyping long prose, everything you need is below. The <em>math</em> is left as <c>???</c> placeholders — read the rendered worksheet (or copy directly from it by right-clicking a math expression and choosing <c>Copy to Clipboard ▸ TeX Commands</c>) and fill each one in as you go.
      </p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Structures you'll use</title>
        <p>
          Each link below opens the template in place — click to see the structure, then switch back to your editor.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ul cols="2">
            <li><p><xref ref="block-worksheet" text="custom">Worksheet and pages</xref></p></li>
            <li><p><xref ref="block-objectives" text="custom">Objectives</xref></p></li>
            <li><p><xref ref="block-definition" text="custom">Definition</xref></p></li>
            <li><p><xref ref="block-exercise-tasks" text="custom">Exercise with tasks</xref></p></li>
            <li><p><xref ref="block-example" text="custom">Worked example</xref></p></li>
            <li><p><xref ref="block-xref" text="custom">Cross-reference</xref></p></li>
            <li><p><xref ref="block-mc" text="custom">Multiple choice</xref></p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <program language="text">
        <![CDATA[
<!-- ---------------- Objectives ---------------- -->

State the product rule for differentiating a product of two functions.

Apply the product rule to compute derivatives of products involving
polynomials, trigonometric, and exponential functions.

Recognize when the product rule is needed, and when it is not.


<!-- --------------- Lead-in paragraph ---------- -->

When a function is built by multiplying two simpler functions, we
cannot just multiply their derivatives. The <term>product rule</term>
tells us what to do instead.


<!-- ----------------- Definition --------------- -->

If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable functions, then
<me>
  ???
</me>


<!-- ------------ Discovery exercise ------------ -->

Title:  Why we need it

Introduction:  Let <m>???</m> and <m>???</m>.

(a) Prompt:  Compute <m>???</m>.
    Answer:   <m>???</m>.

(b) Prompt:  Simplify <m>???</m> first, then differentiate the result
             to find <md>???</md>.
    Answer:   <m>???</m>, so <md>???</md>.

(c) Prompt:  Compare your two answers. Is <m>???</m>?
    Answer:   The answers do not match, so <m>???</m>. We need a
              different rule.


<!-- --------------- Worked example ------------- -->

Title:  Applying the product rule

Statement:  Find the derivative of <m>???</m>.

Solution:   Let <m>???</m> and <m>???</m>, so <m>???</m> and <m>???</m>.
            By <xref ref="def-product-rule"/>,
            <me>
              ???
            </me>


<!-- ------------ Practice exercise ------------- -->

Title:  Practice

Introduction:  Use the product rule (see <xref ref="def-product-rule"/>)
               to find the derivative of each function.

(a) Prompt: <m>???</m>     Answer: <m>???</m>.
(b) Prompt: <m>???</m>     Answer: <m>???</m>.
(c) Prompt: <m>???</m>     Answer: <m>???</m>.
(d) Prompt: <m>???</m>     Answer: <m>???</m>.


<!-- ------------ Multiple-choice --------------- -->

Title:  Spot the derivative

Statement:  Which of the following is the derivative of <m>???</m>?

A.  Statement: <m>???</m>
    Feedback:  It is tempting to take the derivative directly like this,
               but the correct approach is <md>(fg)' = f'g + fg'</md>.

Correct. Statement: <m>???</m>
    Feedback:  With <m>???</m> and <m>???</m>, the product rule gives
               <md>???</md>.

C.  Statement: <m>???</m>
    Feedback:  Close, but the product rule adds the two terms, not
               subtracts them.

D.  Statement: <m>???</m>
    Feedback:  This differentiates only the <m>\sin(x)</m> factor and
               leaves <m>x^2</m> alone. The product rule requires
               differentiating each factor in turn: <m>f'g + fg'</m>.
        ]]>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="bl-bonus">
      <title>If you have time — add a true/false question</title>

      <p>
        Add the question below to the second page of your worksheet. The <xref ref="block-tf" text="custom">true/false template</xref> is close to the multiple-choice pattern, with two small differences: the <c>correct</c> attribute moves onto the <c>&lt;statement&gt;</c> itself, and there is no <c>&lt;choices&gt;</c> block — just a single <c>&lt;feedback&gt;</c>.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-product-rule-tf-demo">
        <title>True or false?</title>
        <statement correct="no">
          <p>
            If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable, then
            <me>
              \frac{d}{dx}\bigl[f(x)\,g(x)\bigr] = f'(x)\,g'(x).
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The correct statement is <m>(fg)' = f'g + fg'</m>. The discovery exercise on page 1 shows why: for <m>f(x) = x^2</m> and <m>g(x) = x^3</m>, the product <m>f'g'</m> gives <m>6x^3</m>, but <m>(fg)'</m> gives <m>5x^4</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="bl-if-stuck">
      <title>If you get stuck</title>

      <p>
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Red error in the preview</title>
            <p>
              Almost always a missing or mismatched tag. The error message usually has a line number. If you can't find it, paste the chunk again and re-edit more carefully.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Preview didn't update</title>
            <p>
              Click <term>Rebuild</term> or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>. The preview doesn't refresh on its own.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Lost your work</title>
            <p>
              Click <term>Projects</term> in the top nav. It's saved.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Something weirder</title>
            <p>
              Raise your hand. Or ask the person next to you first — chances are they've just hit the same thing.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
